          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been anxious for months and it is starting to interfere with daily life. How is anxiety assessed, what does guidance describe as first-line treatment, and where does it sit in ICD-10-CM classification?",
          "answer": "Your anxiety symptoms, which have interfered with daily life for months, are assessed as needing routine care. If your symptoms become severe, worsen, or are new and unexplained, it is essential to seek medical advice.\n\nAnxiety is typically assessed using screening tools, such as the seven-item Generalized Anxiety Disorder questionnaire. This scale helps determine the severity of anxiety symptoms, with specific scores indicating mild, moderate, or severe anxiety (source: \"Generalized anxiety disorder: screening instruments and first-line care\").\n\nGuidance on first-line treatment for anxiety suggests that the primary approaches include psychological therapies like cognitive-behavioral therapy (CBT) and pharmacotherapy with medications such as selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) or serotonin-noradrenaline reuptake inhibitors (SNRIs) (sources: \"Generalized anxiety disorder\" and \"Generalized anxiety disorder: screening instruments and first-line care\"). Non-pharmacological strategies, including structured exercise, relaxation techniques, and mindfulness, can also support anxiety management (source: \"Generalized anxiety disorder\").\n\nIn the ICD-10-CM classification, anxiety disorders are categorized under Chapter 5, which includes mental, behavioral, and neurodevelopmental disorders, specifically ranging from F40 to F48 (source: \"ICD-10-CM Chapter 5: mental, behavioral and neurodevelopmental disorders (F01-F99)\"). This classification encompasses various anxiety-related conditions, such as generalized anxiety disorder, panic disorder, and phobias (source: \"ICD-10-CM Chapter 5\").\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
